# ============================================================================
# SIR — content-type@1.0.5  named export `parse`
# ============================================================================
UNIT parse
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
# Pure function. No clock / RNG / fs / net seam. Read-only: does NOT mutate
# its argument and has no side effects. ORACLE value-mode.

SIG parse(string: string | object) -> { parameters: object, type: string }
# Returns an instance of a private `ContentType` class whose OWN enumerable
# properties are exactly TWO, inserted in THIS ORDER:
#       1st: `parameters`   (created FIRST)
#       2nd: `type`         (assigned SECOND)
# This insertion order is LOAD-BEARING — see the RETURN CONTRACT section. The
# grader compares the returned value via JSON.stringify, which is property-
# ORDER-SENSITIVE, so a re-emitter that returns the same two fields in the
# WRONG order ({ type, parameters }) is semantically identical but FAILS the
# byte-for-byte JSON comparison. `parameters` is a null-prototype object
# (Object.create(null)); `type` is a plain lowercased string.
# On error this function THROWS a TypeError (never returns) — see THROWS.

DEPENDS-ON none
# Zero runtime dependencies. Self-contained regexes + one private class.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RETURN CONTRACT (HARD REQUIREMENT — this is where round 1 failed 0/3)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The original builds the result via a private constructor:
#       function ContentType (type) {
#         this.parameters = Object.create(null)   # <-- parameters set FIRST
#         this.type = type                        # <-- type set SECOND
#       }
# and `parse` does `var obj = new ContentType(type.toLowerCase())`, fills
# `obj.parameters[key] = value` for each param, then `return obj`.
#
# CONSEQUENCE (VERIFIED against the real package — `node -e` below):
#   Object.keys(parse("text/html; charset=utf-8"))  ===  ["parameters","type"]
#   JSON.stringify(parse("text/html; charset=utf-8"))
#       === '{"parameters":{"charset":"utf-8"},"type":"text/html"}'
#   Object.getPrototypeOf(parse("text/html").parameters)  ===  null
#
# RULE 1 (property order — MANDATORY): the returned object's `parameters`
#   property MUST be created/assigned BEFORE its `type` property, so that
#   Object.keys(result) is exactly ["parameters", "type"] and JSON.stringify
#   matches the real package BYTE-FOR-BYTE. Round 1 diverged ONLY here: all
#   three blind emitters were behaviorally correct on every held-out vector
#   but returned `{ type, parameters }` (type first) and the order-sensitive
#   JSON.stringify grader counted them non-equivalent -> 0/3. Do NOT return a
#   `{ type, ..., parameters }` literal (that inserts `type` first).
#
#   CONCRETE EMITTER SHAPE (either form is VERIFIED to yield the right order):
#     (A) object built parameters-first:
#           const obj = { parameters: Object.create(null) };
#           # ... fill obj.parameters[key] = value for each parsed param ...
#           obj.type = type.toLowerCase();      # type assigned LAST
#           return obj;
#     (B) a small class with fields declared parameters-then-type:
#           class ContentType {
#             constructor(type) {
#               this.parameters = Object.create(null);   # FIRST
#               this.type = type;                          # SECOND
#             }
#           }
#           const obj = new ContentType(type.toLowerCase());
#           # ... fill obj.parameters[key] = value ...
#           return obj;
#   In BOTH forms `parameters` is created up front (empty), parameters are
#   then assigned into it, and `type` is assigned LAST — mirroring the source
#   constructor's field order. (Assigning `type` first and `parameters` later,
#   or using a `{ type, parameters }` literal, produces the WRONG key order.)
#
# RULE 2 (parameter insertion order within `parameters`): keys are inserted in
#   SOURCE / scan order (the order the params appear left-to-right in the
#   header). There is NO sorting in `parse` (the sibling `format` sorts, but
#   `parse` does not). VERIFIED:
#     JSON.stringify(parse("text/html; b=2; a=1"))
#       === '{"parameters":{"b":"2","a":"1"},"type":"text/html"}'  # b before a
#   For a duplicate key, the FIRST occurrence fixes the key's POSITION in the
#   object and the LAST occurrence's value wins (plain reassignment keeps the
#   original slot; see NODE 7f).
#
# RULE 3 (null prototype): `parameters` is `Object.create(null)` (no
#   prototype). JSON.stringify and structural key comparison are unaffected by
#   the prototype, but reproduce it for fidelity (and so an emitter never
#   accidentally inherits Object.prototype keys). Empty params -> `{}` in JSON.

DEPENDS-ON none

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXACT CONSTANTS (regexes) — transcribe these BYTE-FOR-BYTE. They are small,
# in-contract literals (NOT carried data). The `u`-less JS source uses \uXXXX
# escapes; reproduce the identical code-point sets.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tchar class (used in type token, param key, and unquoted param value):
#   TCHAR = [!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]
#   (literal char class, exactly: ! # $ % & ' * + . ^ _ ` | ~  digits  A-Z a-z  hyphen)
#
# TYPE_REGEXP = /^[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+\/[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+$/
#   i.e.  ^TCHAR+ "/" TCHAR+$   (one slash, both sides >=1 tchar, NOTHING else;
#   no whitespace, no obs-text high bytes are allowed in the TYPE token.)
#
# PARAM_REGEXP (BYTE-EXACT, from source line 23):
#   /; *([!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+) *= *("(?:[ !#-[]-~-ÿ]|\\[ -ÿ])*"|[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+) */g
#   Structure:  ";"  " "*  ( KEY = TCHAR+ )  " "*  "="  " "*  ( VALUE )  " "*
#     KEY   (capture group 1) = TCHAR+
#     VALUE (capture group 2) = EITHER
#        (a) a quoted-string:  '"'  ( qdtext | quoted-pair )*  '"'
#              qdtext char set =  (vertical tab),   (space),
#                                ! ('!'), #-[ ('#'..'['),
#                                ]-~ (']'..'~'),
#                                -ÿ (obs-text high bytes).
#              NOTE the gap: " ('"') and \ ('\\') are EXCLUDED from
#              qdtext. quoted-pair = '\\' followed by any char in
#               or  -ÿ.
#        (b) a token value: TCHAR+  (no quotes, no backslash, no whitespace).
#   The space runs around ';', '=' are literal SPACE ( ) only via " *".
#   NOTE: " *" matches ZERO-OR-MORE SPACE characters only. (Tabs etc. are NOT
#   matched by these optional-space runs; but the type/header trimming below
#   uses String.prototype.trim which strips all whitespace incl. \t \n \r.)
#   The regex is GLOBAL (/g) and driven via .lastIndex / .exec in a loop.
#
# QESC_REGEXP = /\\([ -ÿ])/g
#   Unescape: replace each backslash + (char in  or  -ÿ) with
#   just that captured char. Applied to a quoted value AFTER stripping the
#   surrounding quotes, ONLY when the value still contains a backslash.
#
# (TEXT_REGEXP, TOKEN_REGEXP, QUOTE_REGEXP exist in the source but are used
#  ONLY by the sibling `format` function — NOT by `parse`. Ignore for parse.)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEHAVIOR  # ordered pipeline — reproduce EXACTLY in this order
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Let `arg` be the single argument.
#
# NODE 1 — falsy guard:
#   IF (!arg)  i.e. arg is any FALSY value
#      ( undefined | null | false | 0 | -0 | 0n | NaN | "" (empty string) )
#   THEN throw new TypeError('argument string is required').
#   [exact message string: "argument string is required"]
#
# NODE 2 — resolve header from object-or-string:
#   IF typeof arg === 'object'  (note: a truthy object; arrays are objects too;
#                                RegExp/Map/Set/Buffer are objects too)
#   THEN header = getcontenttype(arg)   # see NODE 2a
#   ELSE header = arg                    # arg is a primitive that passed NODE 1
#                                        # (a non-empty string, a truthy number,
#                                        #  a truthy bigint, the boolean true, or
#                                        #  a Symbol — though Symbol is out of
#                                        #  codec envelope, see SCOPE).
#
#   NODE 2a — getcontenttype(obj)  (only reached when arg is an object):
#     header2a := undefined
#     IF typeof obj.getHeader === 'function'        # res-like
#        header2a = obj.getHeader('content-type')   # call it with that exact key
#     ELSE IF typeof obj.headers === 'object'       # req-like
#        header2a = obj.headers && obj.headers['content-type']
#        # i.e. if obj.headers is truthy, read obj.headers['content-type'];
#        # if obj.headers is null (typeof null === 'object'), the `&&` short-
#        # circuits to null -> header2a = null.
#     # (else: neither branch -> header2a stays undefined)
#     IF typeof header2a !== 'string'
#        throw new TypeError('content-type header is missing from object').
#        [exact message: "content-type header is missing from object"]
#     RETURN header2a
#   # PRECEDENCE: getHeader is checked FIRST; if present (a function), headers is
#   # never consulted, even when getHeader returns a non-string (which then
#   # throws 'content-type header is missing from object').
#
# NODE 3 — header must be a string:
#   IF typeof header !== 'string'
#   THEN throw new TypeError('argument string is required to be a string').
#   [exact message: "argument string is required to be a string"]
#   # This catches the case where `arg` was a TRUTHY NON-OBJECT, NON-STRING
#   # primitive (e.g. the number 42, the boolean true, a bigint 5n): NODE 1
#   # passed (truthy), NODE 2 took the ELSE branch (header = arg), and arg is
#   # not a string. (For object args, NODE 2a already guaranteed a string or
#   # threw, so this node is a no-op for objects.)
#
# NODE 4 — split type vs parameters at the FIRST ';':
#   index := header.indexOf(';')        # -1 if no semicolon present
#   type  := (index !== -1) ? header.slice(0, index).trim()
#                           : header.trim()
#   # String.prototype.trim strips leading/trailing whitespace
#   # (space, \t, \n, \r, \f, \v, NBSP, etc.) but NOT interior whitespace.
#
# NODE 5 — validate type:
#   IF NOT TYPE_REGEXP.test(type)
#   THEN throw new TypeError('invalid media type').
#   [exact message: "invalid media type"]
#   # Fails for: missing slash, >1 slash that breaks the shape, interior
#   # whitespace surviving trim ("te xt/html"), empty token side, obs-text /
#   # high bytes in the token ("é/é" THROWS here), control chars, etc.
#
# NODE 6 — construct result  (SEE RETURN CONTRACT — order is load-bearing):
#   Create the result object with `parameters` FIRST as a null-prototype object,
#   then (NODE 7) fill params into it, then assign `type` LAST:
#       obj.parameters := Object.create(null)        # CREATED FIRST (empty)
#       # ... NODE 7 fills obj.parameters[key] = value ...
#       obj.type       := type.toLowerCase()         # ASSIGNED LAST; TYPE LOWERCASED
#   The source constructs `new ContentType(type.toLowerCase())` (which sets
#   `this.parameters = Object.create(null)` then `this.type = type`) BEFORE the
#   param loop; functionally identical to creating an empty params object up
#   front and assigning type. What matters for the grader is the final key
#   INSERTION ORDER: parameters BEFORE type. (Do NOT emit a `{type,parameters}`
#   literal — that inserts type first and FAILS the JSON.stringify comparison.)
#
# NODE 7 — parse parameters (ONLY if index !== -1, i.e. a ';' was present):
#   PARAM_REGEXP.lastIndex := index             # start scanning AT the ';'
#   LOOP: while (match = PARAM_REGEXP.exec(header)) is non-null:
#     7a. IF match.index !== index
#            throw new TypeError('invalid parameter format').
#            [exact message: "invalid parameter format"]
#         # Every successful match MUST begin exactly where the previous one
#         # ended (contiguous). A gap (garbage between params, e.g.
#         # "a=b XXX; c=d", or an unparseable region) makes match.index advance
#         # past `index` -> throw. A leading "garbage" before the first ';'
#         # cannot occur because we seeded lastIndex at the ';'.
#     7b. index := index + match[0].length      # advance by the whole match
#     7c. key   := match[1].toLowerCase()        # PARAM KEY IS LOWERCASED
#     7d. value := match[2]                       # raw VALUE (group 2)
#     7e. IF value.charCodeAt(0) === 0x22  (i.e. value starts with a '"'):
#            value := value.slice(1, -1)          # strip the surrounding quotes
#            IF value.indexOf('\\') !== -1:        # only if an escape remains
#               value := value.replace(QESC_REGEXP, '$1')   # unescape \X -> X
#     7f. obj.parameters[key] := value            # VALUE CASE IS PRESERVED
#         # Insertion order = scan order. Duplicate keys: the FIRST occurrence
#         # fixes the key's POSITION; the LAST occurrence's value WINS (plain
#         # assignment overwrites the value but keeps the original key slot).
#   END LOOP
#   7g. AFTER the loop: IF index !== header.length
#          throw new TypeError('invalid parameter format').
#          [exact message: "invalid parameter format"]
#       # i.e. there must be NO leftover trailing characters after the last
#       # matched parameter. A trailing bare ';' ("text/html;") or trailing
#       # junk ("...; ") throws here. A trailing run of SPACES is consumed by
#       # the final " *" of the regex, so trailing spaces after a value are OK
#       # ("text/html; charset=utf-8   " is valid).
#
# NODE 8 — return obj  with own keys in order ["parameters", "type"]
#          (parameters first, type second — see RETURN CONTRACT).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOWERCASING RULE (the load-bearing invariant)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * The media TYPE is lowercased (NODE 6: type.toLowerCase()).
#   * Each parameter KEY is lowercased (NODE 7c: match[1].toLowerCase()).
#   * Parameter VALUES are NOT lowercased — original case is PRESERVED
#     ("TEXT/HTML; Charset=UTF-8"  ->  type "text/html",
#      parameters { charset: "UTF-8" }). Quoted values likewise preserve case.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THROWS — every error path is `throw new TypeError(<message>)`. EXACT strings:
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. "argument string is required"
#        when arg is FALSY (undefined, null, false, 0, -0, 0n, NaN, "").
#   2. "argument string is required to be a string"
#        when arg is a TRUTHY, NON-OBJECT, NON-STRING primitive
#        (number != 0, bigint != 0n, boolean true, Symbol).
#   3. "content-type header is missing from object"
#        when arg is an object (incl. array/RegExp/etc.) and getcontenttype
#        could not resolve a string content-type header (no getHeader fn and no
#        string headers['content-type']; or getHeader returned a non-string).
#   4. "invalid media type"
#        when the trimmed type token fails TYPE_REGEXP (NODE 5).
#   5. "invalid parameter format"
#        when a parameter region is non-contiguous / unparseable (NODE 7a) OR
#        leftover trailing characters remain after the last param (NODE 7g).
#   The throw oracle records the THROWN TypeError's message string. All five
#   messages above are exact and must match byte-for-byte.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMISSION / EXPORT CONTRACT (the grader loads a .mjs via dynamic import())
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Emit an ES MODULE (.mjs) exporting a NAMED export `parse`:
#         export function parse(string) { ... }
#     (or `function parse(...){}; export { parse };`). The grader resolves
#     m['parse'] — a default export will NOT be found. exportName = 'parse',
#     importKind = 'named'.
#   * The original is CommonJS (module.exports.parse = parse); the re-emitter
#     must NOT use module.exports — produce a real ESM named export.
#   * Do NOT require('http') or any module — this unit has zero dependencies.
#   * Use real regex literals with the EXACT \uXXXX code-point classes above.
#   * The returned object's own-key order MUST be ["parameters", "type"]
#     (see RETURN CONTRACT) — the grader's JSON.stringify comparison is
#     order-sensitive; type-first FAILS even when behavior is correct.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire NODE 1..8 pipeline, the
#   getcontenttype object-resolution (getHeader-first, then headers), the three
#   regexes (TYPE_REGEXP, PARAM_REGEXP, QESC_REGEXP) transcribed byte-exact, the
#   lowercasing rule, the quoted-value unescaping, all five exact throw
#   messages, AND the RETURN CONTRACT (parameters-before-type key order, null-
#   prototype parameters, scan-order param insertion). NO carried data — these
#   constants are small in-contract literals.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented; NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   * Symbol argument: NODE 1 (!symbol) is false (truthy), NODE 2 typeof is
#     'symbol' (not 'object') -> header = symbol; NODE 3 throws
#     'argument string is required to be a string'. Symbols do NOT round-trip
#     the JSON codec, so genInputs does not emit one; behavior is documented.
#   * The sibling `format` export and the private getcontenttype/qstring/
#     ContentType helpers are not separately graded; only `parse` behavior.
#   * Inputs whose behavior depends on values that don't survive
#     JSON.stringify(encode(args)) are not emitted (see GENERATOR NOTE). NaN
#     and undefined ARE in the stated codec round-trip set and are exercised.

PROVENANCE content-type@1.0.5  (jshttp/content-type)  MIT License
            source file: index.js  (function `parse`, lines ~106-167; the
            private class `ContentType` lines ~222-225 that fixes the
            parameters-then-type field order; the module-level regex constants
            lines ~23-47; and the private helper getcontenttype lines ~177-193)
